  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1235 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\n12Z upper air analysis continues to show a mid-level ridge\npresent over the Southern Plains and Lower Mississippi Valley. A\nweak quasi-stationary boundary is located from the Ohio Valley,\nbisecting Missouri, and back into the Central and Southern\nPlains. Temperatures across the Lower Mississippi Valley are\nalready in the upper 80s to lower 90s as of 11 AM.\n\nThis upper-level ridge is expected to bring a continuation of\nvery hot temperatures in the upper 90s to around 100 degrees\nthrough Monday ahead of the front with heat index values expected\nto remain below 105 degrees. The aforementioned surface boundary\nis expected to move south as a weak cold front later tonight into\nMonday across the Mid-South as the upper-level ridge weakens\nacross the region. Short-term models indicate mostly isolated\nshowers and thunderstorms developing mainly late Monday afternoon\ninto Monday evening across the area. Overall shear will remain\nweak favoring pulse-type thunderstorms with marginal instability\ncapable of a strong to damaging wind gust at best. This front is\nnot expected to bring any appreciable rainfall to the area.\nAverage rainfall amounts are anticipated to be mainly below one-\ntenth across most areas as probabilities for rainfall amounts\nexceeding one-quarter inch remain low around 20-30 percent.\n\nBehind this front, cooler near-normal to slightly above normal\ntemperatures and a reinforcement of dry air are anticipated with\nhighs in the 80s and lows in the upper 50s to 60s. Long term\nmodels remain in fairly decent agreement into next weekend with\nmainly dry weather expected as an upper level low develops near\nthe Mid-Atlantic states and an upper-level ridge builds back in\nacross the Southern Plains and the Lower Mississippi Valley.",
